  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the sum of all integer bases \\( b > 9 \\) for which the number \\( 17_b \\) is a divisor of the number \\( 97_b \\).\n\n### Step 1: Convert the numbers from base \\( b \\) to base 10\nThe number \\( 17_b \\) in base \\( b \\) can be converted to base 10 as:\n\\[\n17_b = 1 \\cdot b + 7 = b + 7\n\\]\n\nThe number \\( 97_b \\) in base \\( b \\) can be converted to base 10 as:\n\\[\n97_b = 9 \\cdot b + 7 = 9b + 7\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Set up the divisibility condition\nWe need \\( 17_b \\) to be a divisor of \\( 97_b \\), which means:\n\\[\n9b + 7 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{b + 7}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Simplify the congruence\nRearrange the congruence:\n\\[\n9b + 7 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{b + 7}\n\\]\n\\[\n9b + 7 = k(b + 7)\n\\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\n### Step 4: Solve for \\( b \\)\nSubstitute \\( b = k(b + 7) - 9b - 7 \\):\n\\[\nb = kb + 7k - 9b - 7\n\\]\n\\[\nb + 9b = kb + 7k - 7\n\\]\n\\[\n10b = kb + 7k - 7\n\\]\n\\[\n10b - kb = 7k - 7\n\\]\n\\[\nb(10 - k) = 7k - 7\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\frac{7k - 7}{10 - k}\n\\]\n\n### Step 5: Find valid integer values of \\( b \\)\nWe need \\( b > 9 \\) and \\( b \\) to be an integer. Let's test values of \\( k \\) to find valid \\( b \\):\n\n- For \\( k = 1 \\):\n  \\[\n  b = \\frac{7(1) - 7}{10 - 1} = \\frac{0}{9} = 0 \\quad \\text{(not valid)}\n  \\]\n\n- For \\( k = 2 \\):\n  \\[\n  b = \\frac{7(2) - 7}{10 - 2} = \\frac{14 - 7}{8} = \\frac{7}{8} \\quad \\text{(not valid)}\n  \\]\n\n- For \\( k = 3 \\):\n  \\[\n  b = \\frac{7(3) - 7}{10 - 3} = \\frac{21 - 7}{7} = \\frac{14}{7} = 2 \\quad \\text{(not valid)}\n  \\]\n\n- For \\( k = 4 \\):\n  \\[\n  b = \\frac{7(4) - 7}{10 - 4} = \\frac{28 - 7}{6} = \\frac{21}{6} = 3.5 \\quad \\text{(not valid)}\n  \\]\n\n- For \\( k = 5 \\):\n  \\[\n  b = \\frac{7(5) - 7}{10 - 5} = \\frac{35 - 7}{5} = \\frac{28}{5} = 5.6 \\quad \\text{(not valid)}\n  \\]\n\n- For \\( k = 6 \\):\n  \\[\n  b = \\frac{7(6) - 7}{10 - 6} = \\frac{42 - 7}{4} = \\frac{35}{4} = 8.75 \\quad \\text{(not valid)}\n  \\]\n\n- For \\( k = 7 \\):\n  \\[\n  b = \\frac{7(7) - 7}{10 - 7} = \\frac{49 - 7}{3} = \\frac{42}{3} = 14 \\quad \\text{(valid)}\n  \\]\n\n### Step 6: Verify the solution\nCheck if \\( b = 14 \\) satisfies the original condition:\n\\[\n17_{14} = 1 \\cdot 14 + 7 = 21\n\\]\n\\[\n97_{14} = 9 \\cdot 14 + 7 = 126 + 7 = 133\n\\]\n\\[\n133 \\div 21 = 6.333 \\quad \\text{(not an integer, so 14 is not valid)}\n\\]\n\n### Step 7: Re-evaluate the solution\nWe need to find the correct \\( b \\) values. Let's re-evaluate the equation:\n\\[\nb = \\frac{7k - 7}{10 - k}\n\\]\n\nFor \\( k = 7 \\):\n\\[\nb = \\frac{49 - 7}{3} = 14 \\quad \\text{(invalid)}\n\\]\n\nFor \\( k = 6 \\):\n\\[\nb = \\frac{42 - 7}{4} = 8.75 \\quad \\text{(invalid)}\n\\]\n\nFor \\( k = 5 \\):\n\\[\nb = \\frac{35 - 7}{5} = 5.6 \\quad \\text{(invalid)}\n\\]\n\nFor \\( k = 4 \\):\n\\[\nb = \\frac{28 - 7}{6} = 3.5 \\quad \\text{(invalid)}\n\\]\n\nFor \\( k = 3 \\):\n\\[\nb = \\frac{21 - 7}{7} = 2 \\quad \\text{(invalid)}\n\\]\n\nFor \\( k = 2 \\):\n\\[\nb = \\frac{14 - 7}{8} = 0.875 \\quad \\text{(invalid)}\n\\]\n\nFor \\( k = 1 \\):\n\\[\nb = \\frac{7 - 7}{9} = 0 \\quad \\text{(invalid)}\n\\]\n\n### Step 8: Conclusion\nThere are no valid integer bases \\( b > 9 \\) that satisfy the condition. Therefore, the sum of all such bases is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
